{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552680659,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzA5NzQ5OA=="},"target":"6a212ef006d2f62e632a5c2eba8a402f9b3c72064bd053c4ce1cb8913530a019","message":"With revolution upon/within us, I wondered if we could simplify the semantic of having `-d` specification of the dataset in the commands.  At the moment I see it mixing-in at least 2 possible semantics:\n\n### `#1`. How to treat relative paths relative to the top of the specified dataset\n\nThe rationale for this is the consistency with Python use of all those as methods of a dataset instance.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003epeculiarity of `-d .`\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nNote that `-d` should point to the top directory of the dataset, so `-d .` does not mean \"this dataset\", and thus all of the following commands fail, even if we prepend relative path with `./` (which I apparently incorrectly) thought we coded for to describe that it is relative to cwd, not top of the dataset:\n\n```shell\n(git-annex)hopa:/tmp/superds[master]\n$\u003e git status\nOn branch master\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n\n$\u003e mkdir subdir\n\n$\u003e cd subdir\n\n$\u003e touch 123\n\n$\u003e datalad add -d . 123\n[WARNING] path not part of the reference dataset at /tmp/superds/subdir [add(/tmp/superds/subdir/123)] \nadd(impossible): 123 (file) [path not part of the reference dataset at /tmp/superds/subdir]\n\n$\u003e datalad add -d . ./123\n[WARNING] path not part of the reference dataset at /tmp/superds/subdir [add(/tmp/superds/subdir/123)] \nadd(impossible): 123 (file) [path not part of the reference dataset at /tmp/superds/subdir]\n\n$\u003e datalad rev-save -d . ./123   \n[ERROR  ] No installed dataset found at /tmp/superds/subdir. [dataset.py:require_dataset:612] (ValueError)\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\nBut then many commands also add additional semantic beyond \"how to treat relative paths\":\n\n### `#2`. either to consider (only new?) paths to be a part of the dataset\n\nSo `install -d . URL` adds installed dataset, and `install URL` doesn't. \nSimilarly `create` and even `rev-create` (https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/issues/97) would not add new subdataset.\nI no longer remember what was (if any) the rationale for not adding installed or created subdatasets (without -d) right away.  Note that with aforementioned `-d .` gotcha it is actually quite inconvenient to add subdatasets in deep subdirectories within subdatasets.\n\nBecause of this confusion I didn't even reply right away to @kyleam suggestion for a fix in \nhttps://github.com/datalad/datalad-container/issues/72#issuecomment-472925070 since I am not sure what effect that would have, e.g. may be then making behavior different in the top directory of the dataset from how it would behave in subdirectory.\n\nI am not sure if there is yet another specific to command semantic encoded, as I have summarized the 2nd one based on my short-term memory and there might be more?\n\n### Suggestion\n\nBut I wonder if we could get rid of the `#2`, command specific behavior, always figure out the dataset we are in, and behave as it was specified, besides the `#1` aspect of how to treat the provided paths?","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552680625,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIyMDc1MDYzNjU="},"added":["on hold - input required"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1552684332,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzQ0MjY4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473442685"},"message":"Related to #2589.\n\n\u003e even if we prepend relative path with ./ (which I apparently incorrectly) thought we coded for to describe that it is relative to cwd, not top of the dataset:\n\nYou can see that behavior in action with the Python example at #2589.  You can see it on the command-line too if you work around what you noted about `-d.` not resolving to the top-level directory of the repo.\n\nWithout \"./\", the file is taken relative to the specified dataset:\n\n```\n% d=$(mktemp -dt dl-3230-XXX) \u0026\u0026 cd $d\n% datalad create\n% mkdir subdir \u0026\u0026 cd subdir \u0026\u0026 touch foo\n% git rev-parse --show-cdup\n../\n% datalad add -d../ foo\n% (cd ../ \u0026\u0026 git status -uall)\nOn branch master\nUntracked files:\n        subdir/foo\n\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present\n```\n\n\"foo\" doesn't exist in the top-level, so adding it fails (though I was surprised about not getting any message about \"foo\" not existing).\n\nWith \"./\", it is added:\n\n```\n% git rev-parse --show-cdup\n../\n% datalad add -d../ ./foo\nadd(ok): subdir/foo (file)\nsave(ok): /tmp/dl-3230-3Sb (dataset)\naction summary:\n  add (ok: 1)\n  save (ok: 1)\n% (cd ../ \u0026\u0026 git status -uall)\nOn branch master\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552686059,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzQ0OTk5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473449995"},"message":"Oh right, I mixed two issues into one. So the prefixing with ./ had an effect - good. The main issue remains","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552755550,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzU2NTEwMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473565100"},"message":"I don't comprehend the full extend of the implications yet, but I can report that one motive for -revolution was to stop making any assumptions of what a caller probably would have wanted to say and stick to the arguments at their face value.\n\nRational: it is typically easy to construct use cases where some \"decoding\" of semantics in the absence of proof is perfectly straightforward, but it is very hard to confirm that it doesnt lead to mindboggling combinatorics elsewhere.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552759593,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzU3MDk2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473570965"},"message":"But that is exactly my point - a face value of having -d isn't clear ATM, and we do have combinations of effects, which vary across commands. And that is why it is not even clear what implications would be.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1552760760,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzU3MjQ4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473572486"},"message":"Actually, I think it is clear what `-d`is. It is: \"operate on THIS dataset\". The only inconsistency I see is `add`. `create` and `install` behave perfectly fine in that regard, since those commands do not operate on any dataset by default (hence not adding), but bring one into existence. If told to operate on an existing ds, those commands create a subdataset. I find that perfectly fine and reasonable.\n\n`add` is a different story, though. IIRC it has a similar distinction although it would operate on the same dataset both ways. If we are to keep add, then it needs to be reworked.\n\nPath handling is admittedly confusing. And we should iron that out at some point. I agree, but I don't have a solution ATM.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552778723,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzYwMDk5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473600995"},"message":"\u003e Actually, I think it is clear what `-d`is. It is: \"operate on THIS dataset\".\n\nThere is to it if you do specify `-d` + the relative paths resolution, and possibly restrict operation (e.g. if full or relative path points outside of it), or extend it (`add -d . subds/file` would add/save both subds and ds, whenever without `-d` it would add/save only within ds)\n\n\u003e since those commands do not operate on any dataset by default \n\nNot exactly true. If you don't specify `-d` the semantic is \"operate on current dataset or a dataset, to which the specified path belongs\".  That is how all commands, including `add` (on files/directories) operate (you don't need `-d` to add files), even `install subds` installs that subds of the ds you are in. And indeed thus `add`, `create`, and `install` are inconsistent from the rest in adding this additional semantic of \"do not care about (sub)datasets as related to current one, even if explicitly specified on the command line\".  \nSo it is especially strange is the fact that `add subds` doesn't add that subds (even though explicitly specified) without `-d` but goes into subds and adds within it (#3232).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1552822345,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY1Njc5Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473656797"},"message":"WRT `add` we have no disagreement. \n\n\u003e Not exactly true. If you don't specify -d the semantic is \"operate on current dataset or a dataset, to which the specified path belongs\". That is how all commands, including add (on files/directories) operate (you don't need -d to add files), even install subds installs that subds of the ds you are in. \n\nYes. I don't see a contradiction to what I wrote is the meaning of using `-d`. What you just described is the default behavior of those commands. I still don't see how `-d` deviates from my description of what `-d` means. And `install exisiting/path` is different of course. But the difference is not `-d` here. In that case it is not \"creating\" something new. So it behaves the way you described as all commands' default. Looks fine to me.\n\n\u003e adding this additional semantic of \"do not care about (sub)datasets as related to current one, even if explicitly specified on the command line\".\n\nHm. Not clear to me. What do you mean? If explicitly specified they do. Isn't that what you wrote about `install`?\n\u003e So install -d . URL adds installed dataset, and install URL doesn't.\n\n`install something-new` : just install, don't care about \"current\".\n`install -d . something-new` : explicitly specified to care about `.` so - add it.\nSame for create.\n\nI have a feeling you look at `add` as the model. I think it's the outlier. Take it out (like revolution does) and things look consistent to me. So, `add` is the thing to either throw away or rethink entirely. (I agree with revolution and do think we don't need it at all)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552826897,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY2MTIxOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473661218"},"message":"I have spent some thought on the following move (which also results in a disambiguation):\n\n1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, it is only used a) to turn (non-explicit) relative paths into absolute paths, and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\n2. Previous additional behavior like \"install/create and register in a super\" is discontinued and left for an explicit call to `rev-save` in the context and scope of the dataset(-level) that should be saved.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552826897,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzM4NDEyMA=="},"target":"9c9596e58f35c0e7b89f1a9c4b97fe40ff7c122bdd7362cf7022e76b16a7f229","message":"I have spent some thought on the following move (which also results in a disambiguation):\n\n1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, it is only used a) to turn (non-explicit) relative paths into absolute paths, and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\n2. Previous additional behavior like \"install/create and register in a super\" is discontinued (In this specific case, it is left for an explicit call to `rev-save` in the context and scope of the dataset(-level) that should be saved).\n\nI don't see any other option that would work across any command regardless of a command-specific context. Further tailoring therefore needs to happen in the scope and context of a single command (like `rev-save`). This includes any detection of datasets to operate and scoping within them.\n\nWrt the latter issue, I don't think there will be a single standard rule, as we have to live with inherently conflicting behavior. Example: I run `rev-save` in a subdirectory of an unspecified dataset. \"Clearly\" I want to save anything underneath this directory, but not everything throughout the entire dataset. Same setup different command: `rev-status`. \"Clearly\" I want to have `rev-status` behave very similar to `git status`, which is to report the status of the entire dataset, regardless of the PWD.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552827946,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzM4NTg4Nw=="},"target":"9c9596e58f35c0e7b89f1a9c4b97fe40ff7c122bdd7362cf7022e76b16a7f229","message":"I have spent some thought on the following move (which also results in a disambiguation):\n\n1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, it is only used a) to turn (non-explicit) relative paths into absolute paths, and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\n2. Previous additional behavior like \"install/create and register in a super\" is discontinued (In this specific case, it is left for an explicit call to `rev-save` in the context and scope of the dataset(-level) that should be saved).\n\nI don't see any other option that would work across any command regardless of a command-specific context. Further tailoring therefore needs to happen in the scope and context of a single command (like `rev-save`). This includes any detection of datasets to operate and scoping within them.\n\nWrt the latter issue, I don't think there will be a single standard rule, as we have to live with inherently conflicting behavior. Example: I run `rev-save` in a subdirectory of an unspecified dataset. \"Clearly\" I want to save anything underneath this directory, but not everything throughout the entire dataset. Same setup different command: `rev-status`. \"Clearly\" I want to have `rev-status` behave very similar to `git status`, which is to report the status of the entire dataset, regardless of the PWD.\n\nRational for the discontinuation of the \"called on a dataset\" behavior switch: This makes a lot of sense for people that use/know the Python API. There is a binding to a superdataset object and when calling one of its methods, it makes total sense to have it be modified. For everyone else this makes very little sense, or feels a little weird (at least). Even after years I personally keep forgetting to specify `--dataset` to `install` and `create` initially. The decision to implement this behavior originally was motivated by a consistency aim between the Python and cmline API (like the enforcement of argument order). However, there is no object-oriented semantic in the cmdline, and imposing one leads to the problem we are discussion now.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552828010,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzM4NTk5NQ=="},"target":"9c9596e58f35c0e7b89f1a9c4b97fe40ff7c122bdd7362cf7022e76b16a7f229","message":"I have spent some thought on the following move (which also results in a disambiguation):\n\n1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, it is only used a) to turn (non-explicit) relative paths into absolute paths, and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\n2. Previous additional behavior like \"install/create and register in a super\" is discontinued (In this specific case, it is left for an explicit call to `rev-save` in the context and scope of the dataset(-level) that should be saved).\n\nI don't see any other option that would work across any command regardless of a command-specific context. Further tailoring therefore needs to happen in the scope and context of a single command (like `rev-save`). This includes any detection of datasets to operate on and scoping within them.\n\nWrt the latter issue, I don't think there will be a single standard rule, as we have to live with inherently conflicting behavior. Example: I run `rev-save` in a subdirectory of an unspecified dataset. \"Clearly\" I want to save anything underneath this directory, but not everything throughout the entire dataset. Same setup different command: `rev-status`. \"Clearly\" I want to have `rev-status` behave very similar to `git status`, which is to report the status of the entire dataset, regardless of the PWD.\n\nRational for the discontinuation of the \"called on a dataset\" behavior switch: This makes a lot of sense for people that use/know the Python API. There is a binding to a superdataset object and when calling one of its methods, it makes total sense to have it be modified. For everyone else this makes very little sense, or feels a little weird (at least). Even after years I personally keep forgetting to specify `--dataset` to `install` and `create` initially. The decision to implement this behavior originally was motivated by a consistency aim between the Python and cmline API (like the enforcement of argument order). However, there is no object-oriented semantic in the cmdline, and imposing one leads to the problem we are discussion now.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552828040,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzM4NjAyNQ=="},"target":"9c9596e58f35c0e7b89f1a9c4b97fe40ff7c122bdd7362cf7022e76b16a7f229","message":"I have spent some thought on the following move (which also results in a disambiguation):\n\n1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, it is only used a) to turn (non-explicit) relative paths into absolute paths, and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\n2. Previous additional behavior like \"install/create and register in a super\" is discontinued (In this specific case, it is left for an explicit call to `rev-save` in the context and scope of the dataset(-level) that should be saved).\n\nI don't see any other option that would work across any command regardless of a command-specific context. Further tailoring therefore needs to happen in the scope and context of a single command (like `rev-save`). This includes any detection of datasets to operate on and scoping within them.\n\nWrt the latter issue, I don't think there will be a single standard rule, as we have to live with inherently conflicting behavior. Example: I run `rev-save` in a subdirectory of an unspecified dataset. \"Clearly\" I want to save anything underneath this directory, but not everything throughout the entire dataset. Same setup different command: `rev-status`: \"Clearly\" I want to have `rev-status` behave very similar to `git status`, which is to report the status of the entire dataset, regardless of the PWD.\n\nRational for the discontinuation of the \"called on a dataset\" behavior switch: This makes a lot of sense for people that use/know the Python API. There is a binding to a superdataset object and when calling one of its methods, it makes total sense to have it be modified. For everyone else this makes very little sense, or feels a little weird (at least). Even after years I personally keep forgetting to specify `--dataset` to `install` and `create` initially. The decision to implement this behavior originally was motivated by a consistency aim between the Python and cmline API (like the enforcement of argument order). However, there is no object-oriented semantic in the cmdline, and imposing one leads to the problem we are discussion now.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552832046,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY2OTY4NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473669684"},"message":"\u003e 1. Giving `--dataset` does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way, \n\nIn my PoV it does -- (original `#2`) it results in adding subdatasets whenever they aren't added if there is no `-d`.\n\n\u003e ... and b) to identify a dataset to operate on (as a precise instruction, not a random pointer somewhere inside a dataset (to keep the semantics of the Python API).\n\nBut overall -- so you agree that it is 2 aspects which brought together.\nRe \"identify dataset\" -- More than that, at least with the current `add` and `rev-create` ( and not exactly that. It is more of \"identifies the scope\" or \"identifies datasets\" (not a single dataset) to operate on. E.g. `-d superds subds/file` results in adding/saving not only within `subds` but also saving adjusted state within `superds` .\n\nRe `save` vs `status` scoping - makes sense, but not sure how relates to having or not `-d`.\n\n\u003e Rational for the discontinuation of the \"called on a dataset\" behavior switch ...\n\nhere I got a bit lost/confused on either you are agreeing for the need to change or not. Sorry, I might just be slow","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552833702,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY3MTk5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473671990"},"message":"\u003e\u003e Wrt the latter issue, I don't think there will be a single standard rule, as we have to live with inherently conflicting behavior. Example: I run `rev-save` in a subdirectory of an unspecified dataset. \"Clearly\" I want to save anything underneath this directory, but not everything throughout the entire dataset. Same setup different command: `rev-status`: \"Clearly\" I want to have `rev-status` behave very similar to `git status`, which is to report the status of the entire dataset, regardless of the PWD.\n\n\u003e Re `save` vs `status` scoping - makes sense, but not sure how relates to having or not `-d`.\n\nActually - \"hold on\" ;)   If I am just running `rev-save` without restricting by paths, I would expect it to save changes to the entire dataset (similar how `git commit` would do).  And if I want to save this directory only, I specify the `.` path to restrict the scope.  Exactly the same way `datalad|git status` behaves.  I do not see why there could be no \"standard rule\".","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552834807,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY3MzY5MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473673691"},"message":"re @bpoldrack \n\n\u003e WRT add we have no disagreement.\n\nIt seems we talked a bit over each other's head, may be due to my insufficient quoting of the original reply.\nMy point was that in all commands we assume \"current dataset we are in\" to be the dataset we are working on, unless the path is outside the dataset (`-d` does restrict commands to not go outside of that dataset though). And you are describing the current behavior that \"install\" and \"create\" do not operate on any dataset (unless `-d`), and that is what I am \"questioning\" by the discussion here. It all pretty much boils down to how we assume and specify/restrict the scope of commands (hence relates to @mih 's comments), and I feel that there is discrepancy between commands, like the one you stated (`create`, `install` not operating on any dataset by default).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1552838783,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3MzY3OTgyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473679825"},"message":"@yarikoptic \n\u003e It seems we talked a bit over each other's head\n\nYes. I think I got your point now. In my words: You see an inconsistency in `install/create` *without `-d` as compared to other commands, since they then don't make a difference on whether or not PWD is within a ds. I don't find that confusing, but I see how it's not perfect ;-)\n\n\nRe your very first answer to @mih:\n\n\u003e \u003e 1. Giving --dataset does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way,\n\n\u003e In my PoV it does -- (original #2) it results in adding subdatasets whenever they aren't added if there is no -d.\n\u003e ..\n\u003e here I got a bit lost/confused on either you are agreeing for the need to change or not. \n\nI guess now you guys are talking over each other's head. ;-) As I read it, @mih suggests a change. 1. and 2. are outlining how envisions the future, not describing the current state.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1552838783,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzQwNzg1MA=="},"target":"34ffdee8523ddc4df756600aa317dc73f98c1e181bce6270d99e2d5bbc2d0958","message":"@yarikoptic \n\u003e It seems we talked a bit over each other's head\n\nYes. I think I got your point now. In my words: You see an inconsistency in `install/create` *without* `-d` as compared to other commands, since they then don't make a difference on whether or not PWD is within a ds. I don't find that confusing, but I see how it's not perfect ;-)\n\n\nRe your very first answer to @mih:\n\n\u003e \u003e 1. Giving --dataset does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way,\n\n\u003e In my PoV it does -- (original #2) it results in adding subdatasets whenever they aren't added if there is no -d.\n\u003e ..\n\u003e here I got a bit lost/confused on either you are agreeing for the need to change or not. \n\nI guess now you guys are talking over each other's head. ;-) As I read it, @mih suggests a change. 1. and 2. are outlining how envisions the future, not describing the current state.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1552838843,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE5NzQwNzk1Mw=="},"target":"34ffdee8523ddc4df756600aa317dc73f98c1e181bce6270d99e2d5bbc2d0958","message":"@yarikoptic \n\u003e It seems we talked a bit over each other's head\n\nYes. I think I got your point now. In my words: You see an inconsistency in `install/create` *without* `-d` as compared to other commands, since they then don't make a difference on whether or not PWD is within a ds. I don't find that confusing, but I see how it's not perfect ;-)\n\n\nRe your very first answer to @mih:\n\n\u003e \u003e 1. Giving --dataset does not alter the actual behavior of a command in any way,\n\n\u003e In my PoV it does -- (original #2) it results in adding subdatasets whenever they aren't added if there is no -d.\n\u003e ..\n\u003e here I got a bit lost/confused on either you are agreeing for the need to change or not. \n\nI guess now you guys are talking over each other's head. ;-) As I read it, @mih suggests a change. 1. and 2. are outlining how he envisions the future, not describing the current state.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552889528,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3Mzc4MjYzMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473782632"},"message":"FTR: I have lost track of who wants what, and what is a description of a goal rather that the status quo. Let's talk about this in a conference call? Ideally with a concrete proposal.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1552890072,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3Mzc4NDQ0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-473784446"},"message":"Registering one more critical case here: We need to identify a subdataset record in a super vs (the content of) a subdataset itself -- in a way that also works in the Python API. The -revolution commands try to implement:\n\n- `-d super sub` -- the `sub` subdataset record in `super`\n- `-d super sub/` -- the content of sub, even though it is adressed through `super` (like rsync distinguished between a directory vs its content)\n\nAlternatively a subdataset could be addressed standalone (with `-d`), but that would lead to ugly patterns in Python code (we need to get a subdataset object first, and then call its method -- doable, but a fundamental change, and has nasty implications, like having to know what subdatasets exists)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552964564,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ3NDE4MDEwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-474180102"},"message":"\u003e FTR: I have lost track of who wants what, and what is a description of a goal rather that the status quo. Let's talk about this in a conference call? Ideally with a concrete proposal.\n\n+1  I have stopped replying since wanted to get a clear(er) picture as well.  Whatever we decide, the first step of the \"proposal\" in either case should be a description, which would makes it clear of what is the effect(s) having `-d` specified or not.\n\nI am not sure about `sub` vs `sub/`, since its utility is for a limited set of specifications (e.g. a list of directories only at a specific depth) or requires explicit handling to guarantee having `/`, and thus might complicate scripting, where people would need to assure that there is no/is trailing `/` specified.\n\nI still wonder if default context of operation should just be shifted to current dataset (if there is any) as the dataset to operate on, and then `-r` flag would explicitly tell either we are interested in the subdatasets (content or whatever). The gotcha is with outside paths (e.g. `../friendlyds` or by full path). ATM without `-d` the \"context\" just entirely switches to those (sub or outside) datasets, and thus it works just fine, but looses \"connection\" to the dataset where command was launched. Yes - could be considered a feature, but so far it just kept biting me, and hence me whining.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1552964646,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIyMTIyODc3NTI="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569376101,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI2NjAwMjY2MzI="},"added":["relative paths"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569940616,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzNzA2Njc0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3230#issuecomment-537066743"},"message":"according to @mih it is resolved by https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3282","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1569940616,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjY3NzI5OTIzNQ=="},"status":2}]}